{
  "dom_id": "step-q112877",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}9}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$",
